WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.270
- Fear is the chief export of Compton.

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:04.150
It's what everybody knows Compton for,

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:06.680
the fear of drugs, the
fear of gang violence,

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.760
fear of being cut down in the streets,

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.810
fear of black people, fear of black men.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:13.010
These are all things that
are associated with Compton.

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:16.131
When you live a life where
you're fearing walking

00:00:16.131 --> 00:00:17.906
down the street to school,

00:00:17.906 --> 00:00:20.700
or fearing a drive by
happening in at night,

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:21.830
sort of sleeping in your bed,

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:23.920
worrying about bullets
coming through the window,

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.530
I think that fear pushes everybody.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:28.360
I experienced that in a
lot of different ways,

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:32.150
that really impacted how I saw myself

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:33.730
and what it is that I strove for

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:35.463
and what I fought for growing up.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.580
My name is Anthony D. Mays,

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.140
I'm a software engineer at Google.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:49.973
I was not born in Compton,

00:00:49.973 --> 00:00:51.500
no I was born in Omaha, Nebraska,

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:52.960
which is sort of weird for somebody

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:53.930
that's straight outta Compton,

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:55.930
then came to Compton when
I was four years old.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:59.701
When I arrived in 1988, NWA's
Straight Outta Compton album

00:00:59.701 --> 00:01:03.159
had just been released,
Compton was put on the map.

00:01:03.159 --> 00:01:04.356
♪ Straight Outta Compton ♪

00:01:04.356 --> 00:01:06.325
♪ Crazy (bleep) named Ice Cube ♪

00:01:06.325 --> 00:01:08.613
♪ From the gang called
(bleep) With Attitudes ♪

00:01:08.613 --> 00:01:11.580
I began to learn that if
you're gonna live in Compton,

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.790
you sort of have to endure this baptism

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:15.646
of adversity and challenge.

00:01:15.646 --> 00:01:17.560
So I grew up on Mayo Avenue,

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.180
between Green Leaf and Alondra.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:21.980
I had been amazed by the
people that I was seeing,

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:23.480
that sort of quintessential image

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.116
of these old style cars,
booming hip hop music,

00:01:26.116 --> 00:01:29.310
mothers pushing their babies
in strollers down the street,

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.160
dudes just hanging out on the corner.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:32.240
And I found this fascinating,

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:35.241
it was confusing, it was
loud, but it was amazing.

00:01:35.241 --> 00:01:36.790
I grew up on a great street,

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:41.190
but still I had this fear as
a young person of being shot.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.260
Police helicopters, the
ghetto birds, as we call them,

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.890
were always hovering
over our neighborhood,

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:48.780
and so everybody was aware of
what was going on in the city,

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.300
and if even if you
didn't know the details,

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.000
you knew there was
something to be afraid of.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.630
Compton to me is a rose
bush of beauty and pain.

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:58.314
You see people who deal with despair,

00:01:58.314 --> 00:01:59.870
that deal with struggle,

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:01.030
and they're just trying to get by

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:03.345
from day to day, but
you see this resiliency.

00:02:03.345 --> 00:02:05.709
You see the strength,
and you see this courage

00:02:05.709 --> 00:02:07.138
to just keep on persevering.

00:02:07.138 --> 00:02:09.420
I've met some very
beautiful people in Compton

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.740
and I've also had some of
the toughest experiences

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:12.790
of my life in that city.

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:14.190
I first entered foster care

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.100
after I went to elementary school,

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:19.060
my kindergarten teacher
questioned me about some marks

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:20.860
that I had on my neck and on my back,

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:22.626
and at first I told them
that it was a bully,

00:02:22.626 --> 00:02:24.270
I told them it was a bully 'cause

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.120
that's what I was told to say.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:27.480
My teacher didn't believe me

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.281
and when they asked me again,

00:02:29.281 --> 00:02:31.937
I said, yeah well my dad had beat me

00:02:31.937 --> 00:02:34.680
'cause I was being a bad boy.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.810
And they called the police,

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:38.830
police came and picked me up,

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:40.530
took me to Martin Luther King Hospital

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:43.320
where a doctor confirmed that
I had been physically abused.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:44.980
So I was put into foster care.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:46.956
I went and bounced around
to a couple of homes,

00:02:46.956 --> 00:02:49.231
all the while, my would-be foster parents

00:02:49.231 --> 00:02:52.810
had gone to night court
to petition for custody

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:54.490
for me and for my two brothers

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:56.480
who had also gone to
different foster homes.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:58.240
They had been our babysitters.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.419
And so they stood up and
took us all into their home

00:03:00.419 --> 00:03:02.650
out of the kindness and
love of their own heart

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:04.410
they went in and snatched
up three new boys,

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:06.820
three new black boys, and
brought them under their roof.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:09.120
From day one, as soon as we arrived there,

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.540
they said, you don't have to
call us Mr. and Mrs. Croons

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:13.500
anymore, you can call us mom and dad.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.100
And I'll never forget that
because it was at that moment

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:17.530
that we fused as a family.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.298
And not a lot of kids
get that opportunity,

00:03:19.298 --> 00:03:21.130
they've bounced from foster home

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:22.450
to foster home to foster home,

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:23.840
eventually end up homeless.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.400
I was really fortunate and glad

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.510
by the grace of God that
that wasn't my story.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.400
A lot of people that I grew up with,

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.300
you felt like you only had three options.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:33.608
It was either sports, rap music, or drugs.

00:03:33.608 --> 00:03:35.560
I knew that I wasn't
good for those things,

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:37.320
and again I didn't wanna go to jail,

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.916
I knew that doin' drugs got
you in jail, or in the grave.

00:03:39.916 --> 00:03:41.640
But fortunately at my elementary school

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.000
we also had a computer lab,

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.298
and I remember using a
computer for the first time

00:03:45.298 --> 00:03:47.585
and getting to engage
with this technology,

00:03:47.585 --> 00:03:49.860
and it wasn't just
something that was static,

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.557
I could do things and
it would react to me,

00:03:51.557 --> 00:03:52.931
like it was a conversation.

00:03:52.931 --> 00:03:54.857
And that conversation was something

00:03:54.857 --> 00:03:56.410
that I couldn't let go of.

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:58.454
And so for me that was my first experience

00:03:58.454 --> 00:04:02.080
with technology and with
computers, and I loved it.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.167
So I paid attention in class

00:04:03.167 --> 00:04:04.690
and did well with grades

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:06.100
and I just felt like education

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:07.550
was gonna be a better option for me.

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:09.760
I actually experienced
bullying as a result.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.600
They would call me an oreo,
which if you don't know

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.197
what that is, that's a black person

00:04:14.197 --> 00:04:16.685
who is black on the outside
but white on the inside.

00:04:16.685 --> 00:04:18.520
I was getting called Steve Urkle,

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:20.580
because I wore these
thick meta cal glasses.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:23.120
Back then it hurt, I always felt like

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.290
there was a bit of a crabs
in a barrel mentality

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:28.180
where I think some people knew
that I was gonna go somewhere

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:30.677
with my education but in me doing that

00:04:30.677 --> 00:04:33.530
it sort of judges them for not having

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:36.100
that same kind of motivation
or not being able to

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.920
because their circumstance
was different than mine.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.480
My parents tried to
find the best education

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.740
they possibly could for me in Compton.

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:44.589
My school district was being
taken over by the state,

00:04:44.589 --> 00:04:46.801
so I was in a really bad situation.

00:04:46.801 --> 00:04:49.663
In middle school I went to NFL YET,

00:04:49.663 --> 00:04:51.930
which is a school funded by the NFL.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:53.220
And there was a computer lab there,

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:55.610
and the computer lab
administrator was this black guy.

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:57.210
I wanted to go in and play games

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:59.010
and I wanted to go and print rap lyrics,

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:01.090
Bone Thugs N Harmony,
see you at the crossroads

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:03.177
just came out and nobody
knew what they were saying.

00:05:03.177 --> 00:05:05.128
♪ Dead souls, this for Wally ♪

00:05:05.128 --> 00:05:06.783
♪ Eazy, C's Uncle Charlie ♪

00:05:06.783 --> 00:05:08.540
That was like my popularity card.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:11.080
I was the guy that could print
Bone Thugs N Harmony lyrics.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:12.170
The administrator was like look,

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:13.380
if you wanna use these computers,

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:14.840
you need to know how the internet works.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:16.830
You need to know what the WWW means.

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.770
And so I began to learn from him

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:21.160
about how to do these more
advanced things with computers.

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:22.210
I had another mentor,

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:23.850
who was also a computer lab administrator,

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:26.379
he got me and a few other
folks together into a class

00:05:26.379 --> 00:05:28.665
and he just told us like straight up,

00:05:28.665 --> 00:05:30.990
whatever I know, I'll teach you.

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:32.690
He taught me about how to build computers

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:33.810
and how to take 'em apart,

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.530
how to install operating systems

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:37.250
and manage servers, we
were getting to play

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:39.960
with all this fancy tech
that I couldn't afford.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:42.410
By that time I knew that
I was gonna go to college

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:44.010
and get a computer science degree,

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.163
but when I went to high school

00:05:45.163 --> 00:05:47.320
at the California Academy
of Math and Science,

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:48.600
things started to diverge.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:49.610
I started going to school

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:52.090
with people who were really,
really different from me.

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:54.080
Our mascot was a graphing calculator,

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:55.447
so, imagine how ridiculous it is

00:05:55.447 --> 00:05:57.947
for a dude from Compton
to go to a high school

00:05:57.947 --> 00:06:00.280
where the mascot is a graphing calculator.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:02.242
That's also the time when I started

00:06:02.242 --> 00:06:04.860
to experience imposter syndrome.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:06.623
I felt like, am I really smart enough

00:06:06.623 --> 00:06:09.960
to hang with all of these
really smart people?

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:11.220
I just wanted to keep up,

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:13.090
but I always felt like everyone else

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:14.260
was doing better than me,

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:15.767
like they were stronger
and smarter than me,

00:06:15.767 --> 00:06:17.320
and that fear started to come back,

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:19.870
of what if I'm not really able to do this,

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:21.250
what if I don't get into college?

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:23.710
What if I get into college and
then I'm not able to finish?

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:24.830
I felt like I saw other kids

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:28.407
whose parents were
engineers, doctors, lawyers.

00:06:28.407 --> 00:06:30.023
My parents weren't those things.

00:06:30.023 --> 00:06:32.250
I compared myself a lot to other people,

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:34.960
and just didn't feel like
I was on that same level.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.026
It took me a while to understand,

00:06:37.026 --> 00:06:40.170
I was just different, not
any necessarily better

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:41.410
or worse than my peers.

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:43.590
It took me a while to get
used to just being in college.

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:45.220
I just really, really struggled,

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:47.740
and always felt like I was
the only person in my class

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:50.620
that had been through
what I had been through.

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:53.026
And certainly, I was usually
the only black person

00:06:53.026 --> 00:06:55.075
in my classes and so I had to deal

00:06:55.075 --> 00:06:58.024
with the cognitive dissonance
that comes with that.

00:06:58.024 --> 00:07:00.320
I remember being approached by Google,

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:01.760
there was a recruiter on campus

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:04.120
that handed me a pamphlet
and wanted me to apply

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:06.460
for an internship, and I
remember looking at the pamphlet

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:08.266
like, y'all don't want me.

00:07:08.266 --> 00:07:11.108
Y'all don't want no
black man from Compton,

00:07:11.108 --> 00:07:13.450
former foster kid working at Google,

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:15.500
and certainly I'd never seen black people

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:17.002
perceived as software engineers

00:07:17.002 --> 00:07:20.940
in movies and in TV shows so
I just didn't have the notion

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:22.394
of what it would look like for me

00:07:22.394 --> 00:07:24.760
to work at a place like Google.

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:27.134
So I threw that application away,

00:07:27.134 --> 00:07:31.500
graduated from UC Irvine
on the five year plan,

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:33.130
bounced around to a couple of jobs,

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:35.717
Google caught me in 2011,
thirteen years later or something,

00:07:35.717 --> 00:07:38.360
I remember I was so excited
I ran around my house

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:39.640
like three or four times,

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:41.680
tried as best as I could
to study and prepare,

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:43.579
I had heard that Google tested candidates

00:07:43.579 --> 00:07:45.080
based on brain teasers,

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:46.850
so I studied brain teasers for two weeks,

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:48.418
turns out that was a complete lie,

00:07:48.418 --> 00:07:49.990
and I was totally unprepared.

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:51.400
The Google interview didn't go well,

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:52.930
and I really struggled with that,

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:55.360
because when people found
out that I had applied,

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:56.800
they were like cheering me on,

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:58.477
they were like "oh Anthony
if anybody can make it

00:07:58.477 --> 00:07:59.810
"to Google, we know it's you".

00:07:59.810 --> 00:08:01.880
I had people at church
that were praying for me,

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:03.720
when I got the no from my recruiter,

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:05.230
I had to go back and tell those people,

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:06.930
no I didn't get in, and I felt like,

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:09.447
it wasn't just that I had let myself down,

00:08:09.447 --> 00:08:12.370
but that I let down a
whole community of people.

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:14.114
And so when Google called in 2012,

00:08:14.114 --> 00:08:17.130
called me again to
interview, I told them no.

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:19.580
Because I didn't wanna go
through the embarrassment

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:20.470
of failing again.

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:22.462
Fortunately Google
called me again in 2013,

00:08:22.462 --> 00:08:24.685
which was just ridiculous,
'cause who does that?

00:08:24.685 --> 00:08:26.490
And when I got that phone call

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:27.665
I was like I gotta try this again.

00:08:27.665 --> 00:08:29.980
While I was on the bus and
while I was on the train,

00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:32.110
I would have a little
note pad and a pencil

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:33.558
to study programmer problems,

00:08:33.558 --> 00:08:35.420
and I was studying that like every day,

00:08:35.420 --> 00:08:37.300
like three hours a day, four hours a day,

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:38.760
I was studying at lunch,

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:40.980
I would study before work,
I would study after work,

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:42.770
I would make my wife practice with me.

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:45.468
I knew exactly the day that
the recruiter was gonna call me

00:08:45.468 --> 00:08:47.640
and tell me whether I got an offer or not.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:49.450
I was on pins and needles the whole day,

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:51.048
like I felt like I was just gonna die.

00:08:51.048 --> 00:08:52.753
I remember when the phone rang,

00:08:52.753 --> 00:08:54.730
I almost jumped out of my socks,

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:56.590
like I almost had a heart
attack and fell over.

00:08:56.590 --> 00:08:58.137
And the recruiter was like,

00:08:58.137 --> 00:08:59.765
"hey I've got somebody on the phone,

00:08:59.765 --> 00:09:01.640
"do you know who Laszlo Bock is?"

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:04.190
Who's the head of People
Operations for Google at the time,

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:05.681
and I was like okay great, I failed,

00:09:05.681 --> 00:09:08.821
and they've sent in the big
guns to let me down easy.

00:09:08.821 --> 00:09:11.327
A week before, I had sent
a letter to my recruiters

00:09:11.327 --> 00:09:14.026
thanking them and I was
just expressing my gratitude

00:09:14.026 --> 00:09:15.613
for having made it that far,

00:09:15.613 --> 00:09:18.810
turns out that that letter
ended up getting forwarded on

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:20.050
to the executive committee.

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:22.408
And Laszlo was like "yeah,
so we got your letter,

00:09:22.408 --> 00:09:25.213
"and I'm just proud to be able to tell you

00:09:25.213 --> 00:09:28.570
"that we'd love to extend
an offer to you at Google".

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:29.930
Everything that he said after that

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:31.500
pretty much turned into noise

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:33.440
'cause I couldn't understand anything,

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:35.250
like I just, I couldn't
even feel my fingers

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:36.820
and it was a moment where I felt like

00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:39.920
I had achieved what I was
put on Earth to achieve.

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:42.009
And so I called my wife, my parents,

00:09:42.009 --> 00:09:44.680
I told my kids and they
sang me a little song,

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:47.180
they were like, "Happy Google to you".

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:49.417
People at my church were shedding tears,

00:09:49.417 --> 00:09:52.480
I was just floored by it
and humbled by the reaction.

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:53.907
I think about those moments all the time,

00:09:53.907 --> 00:09:56.180
and I think about the
people that I represent

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:57.520
and the community that I come from,

00:09:57.520 --> 00:09:58.920
and about how important it is for me

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:01.365
to do a good job, to do good work,

00:10:01.365 --> 00:10:05.080
to teach others what I've
learned, to pay that back.

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:06.370
It's good to have that fire

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:08.617
and to keep moving that forward.

00:10:08.617 --> 00:10:10.689
And it helps me sort of
to deal with the fact

00:10:10.689 --> 00:10:15.689
that I made it out, when a
lot of other friends didn't.

00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:18.065
I had a friend of mine,
we were the same age,

00:10:18.065 --> 00:10:20.092
and when he made it to 19 years old

00:10:20.092 --> 00:10:22.100
he was shot and killed, and I always think

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:24.286
about like what was so
different about him and me,

00:10:24.286 --> 00:10:26.020
that I made it out and that he didn't?

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:27.947
And I don't think that there was anything

00:10:27.947 --> 00:10:29.670
really different about us.

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:30.823
I believe that,

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:42.614
I believe that I didn't
deserve to make it out

00:10:42.614 --> 00:10:44.861
any more that he did, I could've died

00:10:44.861 --> 00:10:47.650
just like he did, but I didn't.

00:10:47.650 --> 00:10:50.000
I don't think that I'm
the hero of my own story,

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.355
I think God is the hero of my story,

00:10:51.355 --> 00:10:53.010
I'm just along for the ride,

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:54.460
but because I'm in this position

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:57.177
I have a responsibility
to make the best of this.

00:10:57.177 --> 00:10:58.599
I carry that burden with me,

00:10:58.599 --> 00:11:01.690
because I know that if I
don't do this, who else will?

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:03.130
As a software engineer at Google,

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:05.251
I help to maintain software systems,

00:11:05.251 --> 00:11:07.600
particularly around data visualization,

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:08.820
which is a fancy way of saying

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:10.820
I help people make charts and graphs.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:12.065
At Google we have tons of data,

00:11:12.065 --> 00:11:13.370
and it's really important

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:14.607
that people be able to understand that.

00:11:14.607 --> 00:11:16.720
And I love being able to build tools

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:18.840
that are used by people
all over the world.

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:21.057
I remember for a time I felt
uncomfortable in my seat,

00:11:21.057 --> 00:11:23.950
like those first three or four months

00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:25.090
and I couldn't figure it out.

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:27.130
The office at the time I
would look out the window

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:28.500
and there was a basketball court.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:30.760
And the basketball court
would sort of put me at ease,

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:33.316
and I couldn't figure out why
I just felt this uneasiness.

00:11:33.316 --> 00:11:37.850
Until Google released their
diversity numbers in 2014,

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:40.370
and had sort of admitted to the world,

00:11:40.370 --> 00:11:43.650
that two percent of employees
at Google are black,

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:45.980
and only one percent
of employees at Google

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:47.840
are black and in a technical role.

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:49.180
All throughout my career,

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:51.272
I've known about something
called the black tax.

00:11:51.272 --> 00:11:52.995
And the black tax is this idea

00:11:52.995 --> 00:11:55.489
that as a black person working in America,

00:11:55.489 --> 00:11:58.060
you have to work twice
or three times as hard

00:11:58.060 --> 00:11:59.440
as someone who's not black

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:00.840
to get as far as they might.

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:02.470
So for me I'm always in that mode

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:06.423
of defending who I am as a
professional and as a black man.

00:12:06.423 --> 00:12:08.670
So at Google it was the same sort of deal.

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:11.142
Like, work extra hard and try
to do as much as possible.

00:12:11.142 --> 00:12:13.579
And that's when this light
bulb went off in my head,

00:12:13.579 --> 00:12:16.100
and I looked to my left
and looked to my right

00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:18.271
and realized like I'm one
of only three black people

00:12:18.271 --> 00:12:20.808
in my office of like 400 people.

00:12:20.808 --> 00:12:23.850
From that moment onwards,
I began to commit myself

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:25.779
to helping to do what I could

00:12:25.779 --> 00:12:28.029
to improve diversity
and inclusion in tech.

00:12:28.029 --> 00:12:30.120
In helping to even more aggressively

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:32.565
pay forward everything that I had learned,

00:12:32.565 --> 00:12:35.072
and gained in my own career in life.

00:12:35.072 --> 00:12:37.064
If you're in Harlem, Chicago,

00:12:37.064 --> 00:12:38.490
in Africa, if you're in India,

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:40.780
wherever you are, you can do this too,

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:43.690
if you're willing to put in
the work and spend the time.

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:46.273
(mellow music)

